{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>My son's tunic.</b> That is, this is my son's tunic. <b>A wild beast has devoured him.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): if bandits had killed him, they would have taken the tunic." "<b>And by way of the Midrash</b> — 'A wild beast has devoured him': the holy spirit flickered within him, that in the end the wife of Potiphar would entangle him. And why did the Holy One, blessed be He, not reveal it to him? Because they imposed a ban and cursed anyone who would reveal it to Jacob, and they included the Holy One, blessed be He, with them — so it is written in Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer. The meaning of the matter is that they were nine, and therefore they included the Holy One, blessed be He, to complete the number ten, and they placed the matter under ban. And let this not be difficult in your eyes, for so did Abraham do in the matter of Sodom: he sought the joining of the Holy One, blessed be He, with nine for each and every city, saying to Him, 'You are the Righteous One of the world — join with them and they will be saved.' And if you should say, this is not comparable, for that was for salvation and this is for calamity — this is not calamity but rather the salvation of all Israel and glory for the Holy One, blessed be He, for they feared for their lives, that if, God forbid, the matter were revealed to Jacob, he would curse them with an everlasting curse, and Israel, who depend on the seed of Jacob, would be obliterated from the world, and there would be none to proclaim the divinity, as it says (Joshua 7): ״והכריתו את שמנו מן הארץ ומה תעשה לשמך הגדול״, 'and they will cut off our name from the earth, and what will You do for Your great name.' Therefore it appeared to them that the joining and the partnership were a compelling necessity — a need of the Most High and the salvation of all Israel. And so we find with Moses that he sought the joining of the Holy One, blessed be He, and His court with him in the receiving of the Torah. And if perhaps you ask, how did Isaac know — as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: ״ואביו״ and not the father of his father — one may say that the Holy One, blessed be He, told him; and so He told Benjamin, who knew through the holy spirit, for the stone of Benjamin speaks, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded ״יש פה״ — 'there is a mouth here'; yet despite this he was silent and did not reveal it, because of the ban, for all nine and the Holy One, blessed be He, joined with them had agreed that the matter should not be revealed to Jacob — for the concern was only about Jacob, lest he curse them, as I have mentioned."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:18:31.500968852Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}